/* Lesson quizzes — batch 1 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p1m1":[
 {id:"p1m1q1", q:"Without a calculator, convert 201 to binary and to hex. Which pair is correct?", options:["10010011 and 0x93","11001001 and 0x9C","11001001 and 0xC9","11000101 and 0xC5"], answer:2, why:"201 = 128 + 64 + 8 + 1, so the bits are 11001001; split into nibbles, 1100 is C and 1001 is 9, giving 0xC9. 10010011 is what you get by reading the repeated-division remainders top-down instead of bottom-up."},
 {id:"p1m1q2", q:"A customer's fiber plan is 500 Mb/s. Ignoring protocol overhead, what is the shortest possible time to download a 45 GB game?", options:["About 12 minutes","About 90 seconds","About 96 minutes","About 6 minutes"], answer:0, why:"Network speeds are in bits: 500 Mb/s / 8 = 62.5 MB/s, and 45,000 MB / 62.5 MB/s = 720 s, or 12 minutes. 90 seconds treats megabits as megabytes, missing the factor of eight between b and B."},
 {id:"p1m1q3", q:"You want a 24B-parameter model to run entirely on a 24 GB GPU, keeping about 4 GB free for the KV cache and the display. What is the highest-precision weight format that works?", options:["BF16, since it keeps FP32's range at half the size","FP8, since one byte per weight just fits in 24 GB","FP16, since consumer GPUs are built around it","4-bit, which leaves roughly 10 GB to spare"], answer:3, why:"Weights = parameters x bytes each: BF16 and FP16 need 48 GB, FP8 needs 24 GB with nothing left for the KV cache or display, and 4-bit needs about 12-14 GB with its scale factors. Only 4-bit fits with the required headroom."},
 {id:"p1m1q4", q:"A debugger shows the byte 0xF6 in a variable declared as an 8-bit signed integer. What value does the program see?", options:["246","-10","-118","-9"], answer:1, why:"0xF6 is 11110110. In two's complement the top bit is worth -128, so -128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 4 + 2 = -10 (check: flip to 00001001 and add 1, giving 10). 246 is the unsigned reading; -118 treats the top bit as a plain minus sign."},
 {id:"p1m1q5", q:"A signal passes through a chain of 1,000 CMOS gates, and every wire picks up about 0.1 V of noise, yet the bit arrives as clean as it started. Why?", options:["On-chip wires are shielded, so noise never reaches the 0.3 V threshold","Each gate adds a parity check that lets the next gate detect and fix errors","Each gate re-drives a clean full-level output, so noise never adds up","Binary signals switch too quickly for noise to change their value"], answer:2, why:"A sagging 0.85 V input is still above the 0.7 V threshold, and the gate outputs a clean 1.0 V, so noise is wiped out at every stage instead of accumulating as in an analog chain. Parity and ECC protect stored or transmitted data, not each gate."}
],
"p1m2":[
 {id:"p1m2q1", q:"A 4-bit ALU adds 1100 and 0110. Which statement about the result and the C and V flags is correct?", options:["Result 0010; C = 1 and V = 1, so both readings of the sum are wrong","Result 1010; C = 0 and V = 1, so only the signed reading is wrong","Result 0010; C = 0 and V = 0, because the carry out of the top column is simply discarded","Result 0010; C = 1 (unsigned sum wrong) and V = 0 (signed -4 + 6 = 2 right)"], answer:3, why:"12 + 6 = 18 does not fit in four bits, so a carry leaves the top column and C = 1. The carry into the top column (1) equals the carry out (1), so V = 0: read as signed, -4 + 6 = +2 is exactly right. Confusing C with V is the classic signed/unsigned bug."},
 {id:"p1m2q2", q:"An alarm must output 1 when A is 1 and at least one of B or C is 1. Building it from the truth table's 1-rows, which simplified expression is right?", options:["A + BC","AB + BC + AC","A(B + C)","ABC"], answer:2, why:"The 1-rows are 101, 110 and 111; AB'C + ABC' + ABC simplifies to AB + AC = A(B + C). AB + BC + AC is the majority voter, which wrongly fires for 011, when A is 0, and A + BC fires for 100."},
 {id:"p1m2q3", q:"You have only 74HC00 chips (four 2-input NAND gates each). Which wiring produces A OR B?", options:["(A NAND B) NAND (A NAND B)","(A NAND A) NAND (B NAND B)","(A NAND B) NAND A","A NAND B alone, since De Morgan makes it an OR"], answer:1, why:"A NAND A is NOT A, so the circuit computes NOT(NOT A AND NOT B), which De Morgan's law turns into A OR B. NANDing a NAND output with itself only inverts it, giving AND; a lone NAND is the OR of the inverted inputs."},
 {id:"p1m2q4", q:"You raise a CPU from 5.0 to 5.6 GHz without changing voltage. It boots, but a stress test reports wrong results and then the system crashes. What is happening at gate level?", options:["The slowest path no longer settles within 1/f, so flip-flops capture half-settled values","The core quietly drops back to 5.0 GHz whenever its gates fall behind the clock","Higher frequency raises leakage, which drains the stored bits out of the registers","The adder runs out of carry-lookahead logic and falls back to ripple carry"], answer:0, why:"At 5.6 GHz each stage gets about 179 ps instead of 200 ps; if the critical path needs longer, the flip-flop latches a wrong value. Logic does not wait for slow gates, so an unstable overclock gives silent errors, not a slower computer. More voltage buys margin at a cost growing with V squared."},
 {id:"p1m2q5", q:"Clock-gating idle blocks of a CMOS chip cuts their power to almost nothing, even though their transistors stay connected to the supply. Why does that work?", options:["Each gate passes a steady current from supply to ground, which clock gating switches off","CMOS draws current mainly when it switches, charging wire capacitance, so power tracks C·V²·f","Idle gates settle into the forbidden band, which draws less current than a full 1 or 0","An output held at 1 draws current while a 0 does not, and idle blocks hold mostly 0s"], answer:1, why:"In either steady state one transistor of each complementary pair is off, so only leakage flows; energy is spent charging and discharging capacitance on each transition. Stop the clock and the transitions stop. A CMOS gate is not a light bulb with a constant current path."}
],
"p1m3":[
 {id:"p1m3q1", q:"Which kit has the lower first-word latency: DDR5-6400 CL40 or DDR5-5200 CL26?", options:["DDR5-6400 CL40, since higher MT/s always means lower latency","DDR5-5200 CL26: about 10 ns against 12.5 ns","They tie at about 10 ns, because CL scales with speed","DDR5-5200 CL26: about 5 ns against 6.25 ns"], answer:1, why:"Divide CL by the memory clock, which is half the transfer rate: 26 / 2,600 MHz = 10 ns and 40 / 3,200 MHz = 12.5 ns. Dividing by the MT/s figure halves both answers. Higher MT/s buys bandwidth, not necessarily lower latency."},
 {id:"p1m3q2", q:"You copy 300 GB to a TLC NVMe drive rated at 6,000 MB/s. Its SLC cache absorbs 120 GB; after that, writes run at 1,200 MB/s. About how long does the copy take?", options:["About 50 seconds","About 4 minutes 10 seconds","About 2 minutes 50 seconds","About 1 minute 25 seconds"], answer:2, why:"The first 120,000 MB go at 6,000 MB/s (20 s); the remaining 180,000 MB at 1,200 MB/s take 150 s, about 170 s in all. 50 s assumes the box speed lasts the whole copy, but it holds only until the SLC cache fills."},
 {id:"p1m3q3", q:"A 2 TB SSD is rated for 1,200 TBW. A video editor writes about 200 GB to it every day. Roughly how long before that rated endurance is used up?", options:["About 16 years","About 6 years","About 1.6 years","About 5 years, the length of the warranty"], answer:0, why:"1,200 TB / 0.2 TB per day = 6,000 days, about 16.4 years. Dividing 1,200 by 200 without converting GB to TB gives 6, a units slip. The warranty term limits the guarantee, not the wear on the cells."},
 {id:"p1m3q4", q:"A small-office NAS has four 8 TB drives and must survive the failure of any two drives. Which configuration meets that, and how much usable space does it give?", options:["RAID 10, with 16 TB usable","RAID 5, with 24 TB usable","RAID 0, with 32 TB usable","RAID 6, with 16 TB usable"], answer:3, why:"RAID 6 stores double parity, giving n - 2 = 16 TB and surviving any two failures. RAID 10 also gives 16 TB, but it loses data if both drives of the same mirror pair fail. Neither replaces a separate backup."},
 {id:"p1m3q5", q:"After enabling EXPO on a new DDR5 build, the PC crashes with a different error each time and some downloaded archives fail their checksums. What is the best next step?", options:["Replace the SSD, since corrupted archives point to failing flash","Run MemTest86 with EXPO on, then again with it off","Reinstall Windows, since varied stop codes mean driver corruption","Free up SSD space, since a full SLC cache corrupts written files"], answer:1, why:"Varied crashes plus silently corrupted data are the classic signature of unstable RAM, and the profile was the last change. Testing with it on and off isolates the cause. A failing SSD shows S.M.A.R.T. errors; a full SLC cache slows writes but does not corrupt them."}
],
"p1m4":[
 {id:"p1m4q1", q:"A program executes 10 billion instructions. Chip A runs it at 5 GHz with an average CPI of 0.5; chip B runs it at 4 GHz with an average CPI of 0.35. Which finishes first?", options:["Chip B: about 0.875 s against 1.0 s for chip A","Chip A, since 5 GHz beats 4 GHz on the same ISA","They tie, since both run the same instruction count","Chip A: about 4 s against about 7 s for chip B"], answer:0, why:"Time = instructions x CPI x clock period: A takes 10 billion x 0.5 x 0.2 ns = 1.0 s; B takes 10 billion x 0.35 x 0.25 ns = 0.875 s. Clock speed is only one of three levers. The 4 s and 7 s figures come from using IPC where CPI belongs."},
 {id:"p1m4q2", q:"A compare instruction sets the Z flag, and the next instruction is JZ loop_start (jump if zero). What does the jump actually change?", options:["The stack pointer, which saves a return address as a call does","The flags register, which is cleared before execution continues in order","The instruction register, which the decoder reloads straight from loop_start","The program counter, which is overwritten with loop_start's address"], answer:3, why:"The jump reads the Z flag and, because it is set, overwrites the PC; the next fetch then comes from loop_start, which is how a loop jumps backward. Only call and ret use the stack, and the instruction register is filled by the next fetch, not by the jump."},
 {id:"p1m4q3", q:"A new AI tool dies instantly with 'Illegal instruction' on a 2011-era x86-64 desktop but runs fine on a recent x86-64 laptop. What is the most likely cause?", options:["It was compiled for ARM64, so the desktop cannot decode it","The desktop runs a 32-bit OS that cannot address enough memory","It uses a vector extension such as AVX2 that the older CPU lacks","The desktop's outdated microcode blocks newly released software"], answer:2, why:"Both machines are x86-64, so an ARM64 build would fail on both. An instruction-set extension the old CPU does not implement (AVX2 arrived in 2013) faults as an illegal instruction the moment it executes; check the CPU flags with CPU-Z or /proc/cpuinfo."},
 {id:"p1m4q4", q:"A laptop has 8 GB of RAM, which passes MemTest86, but the system information page reports only about 3.4 GB usable. What is the most likely cause?", options:["One of the two 4 GB modules has failed and been disabled","A 32-bit operating system, with devices claiming part of the 4 GiB space","The integrated GPU has permanently reserved 4.6 GB of system memory","Dual-channel mode is off, so only one channel's memory is visible"], answer:1, why:"A 32-bit OS can address at most 4 GiB, and device address windows take part of that, leaving roughly 3.2-3.5 GB. A disabled module would leave about 4 GB, not 3.4, and single-channel mode costs bandwidth, not capacity."},
 {id:"p1m4q5", q:"You move a local-LLM project from your x86-64 RTX laptop to an ARM64 Mac Studio. Which part runs on the Mac without being rebuilt or replaced?", options:["The Python source code and the model weight files","The CUDA kernels, which Rosetta 2 translates at install","The compiled x86-64 llama.cpp binary with its CUDA backend","The NVIDIA driver, once a macOS version is installed"], answer:0, why:"Source code and weight files are portable data. Rosetta 2 translates x86-64 CPU code, not GPU kernels, and CUDA needs an NVIDIA GPU, so on the Mac the model runs through Metal, MLX or llama.cpp's Metal backend instead."}
],
"p1m5":[
 {id:"p1m5q1", q:"You are choosing a cooler for an AMD Ryzen CPU with a 105 W TDP, run at stock settings. What sustained package power should the cooler be able to handle?", options:["105 W, since TDP is the chip's power draw","About 210 W, since AMD's limit is twice the TDP","About 142 W, the PPT limit of 1.35 x TDP","About 78 W, since TDP already includes headroom"], answer:2, why:"AMD's socket limit is Package Power Tracking, 1.35 x TDP, so about 142 W at stock. TDP is a cooler-design guidance figure, not a measurement; sizing to 105 W invites throttling under all-core load. Doubling resembles Intel's PL2, not AMD's PPT."},
 {id:"p1m5q2", q:"L1 hits take 1 ns with a 10% miss rate. L2 hits take 5 ns, and 30% of the requests that reach L2 go on to DRAM at 90 ns. What is the average memory access time?", options:["About 10 ns","About 4.2 ns","About 28.5 ns","About 3.2 ns"], answer:1, why:"AMAT = 1 + 0.10 x (5 + 0.30 x 90) = 1 + 0.10 x 32 = 4.2 ns. 28.5 ns applies the DRAM term to every access, forgetting that only L1 misses reach L2; 10 ns skips L2; 3.2 ns drops the L1 hit time every access pays."},
 {id:"p1m5q3", q:"A loop executes 1 billion branches on a 5 GHz core. 4% are mispredicted, and each mispredict costs 18 cycles. How much time do the mispredicts add?", options:["About 3.6 s","About 0.72 s","About 0.008 s","About 0.14 s"], answer:3, why:"1 billion x 0.04 = 40 million mispredicts x 18 cycles = 720 million cycles; at 0.2 ns per cycle that is 0.144 s. 0.008 s forgets the 18-cycle penalty, 0.72 s uses 1 ns cycles, and 3.6 s charges every branch as mispredicted."},
 {id:"p1m5q4", q:"A CPU sustains 4.8 GHz at 1.20 V and draws 180 W. Holding 5.2 GHz needs 1.32 V. Using dynamic power ≈ C·V²·f, about how much will it draw?", options:["About 236 W","About 195 W","About 218 W","About 215 W"], answer:0, why:"(1.32/1.20)² x (5.2/4.8) = 1.21 x 1.083 = 1.31, so about 236 W: 31% more power for 8% more clock. 195 W scales only frequency, 218 W only voltage squared, and 215 W forgets to square the voltage."},
 {id:"p1m5q5", q:"A game runs at 97 fps at 1080p low and 94 fps at 4K ultra on the same PC. What is the most likely limit, and what would a faster GPU do?", options:["The GPU, so a faster card would raise the 4K frame rate a lot","VRAM capacity, so a card with more memory would fix both","The CPU, so a faster GPU would barely change the frame rate","The display, since 97 fps is above what most monitors show"], answer:2, why:"Raising the resolution multiplies the GPU's work, yet the frame rate barely moved, so the GPU has headroom and the CPU, usually one busy thread, sets the pace. A GPU-bound game would drop sharply at 4K ultra."}
],
"p1m6":[
 {id:"p1m6q1", q:"During GPU-Z's render test, a desktop graphics card on a vertical-mount riser cable reports 'PCIe 5.0 x16 @ x8 4.0'. What does that tell you?", options:["Normal power saving; the link climbs to 5.0 x16 once it is under load","The link trained down, likely at the riser, to 15.8 GB/s each way, not 63 GB/s","The slot is bifurcated x8/x8 by design, and each half still delivers the full 63 GB/s","The card's own VRAM bandwidth has fallen to a quarter of its rating"], answer:1, why:"Under load the link should run 5.0 x16. Gen 4 x8 is 16 GT/s x 128/130 / 8 x 8 lanes = 15.8 GB/s, a quarter of 63 GB/s; a marginal riser or dusty slot makes training settle lower. The power-saving drop happens only at idle, and VRAM bandwidth is inside the card."},
 {id:"p1m6q2", q:"On an AM5 board, a PCIe 5.0 SSD rated at 14,000 MB/s goes into an M.2 slot the manual lists as chipset-attached. What is the most it can deliver?", options:["About 8 GB/s at most, shared over the chipset's PCIe 4.0 x4 uplink","The full 14 GB/s, since every M.2 slot connects directly to the CPU","About 16 GB/s, limited by the chipset's DMI 4.0 x8 link","About 2 GB/s, since the chipset link is a single PCIe 4.0 lane"], answer:0, why:"On AM5 everything behind the chipset shares one PCIe 4.0 x4 uplink, about 7.9 GB/s, together with USB, networking and SATA. Only a CPU-attached M.2 slot lets the drive approach its rating. DMI 4.0 x8 is Intel's LGA 1851 chipset link, not AMD's."},
 {id:"p1m6q3", q:"A mini PC runs a 4-bit 8B model (about 5 GB of weights) on its CPU from a single DDR5-5600 stick. What is the generation-speed ceiling, and what does a matched second stick in the other channel do?", options:["About 9 tokens/s, unchanged by a second stick, because capacity is not the limit","About 18 tokens/s, rising to about 36 with the second stick","About 4.5 tokens/s, since each DDR5 subchannel is only 32 bits wide","About 9 tokens/s, roughly doubling to 18 with the second stick"], answer:3, why:"One channel moves 5,600 MT/s x 8 bytes = 44.8 GB/s; reading 5 GB per token gives about 9 tokens/s. Dual channel doubles bandwidth to 89.6 GB/s, so about 18. Decoding is bandwidth-bound, which is why two sticks beat one even when capacity suffices."},
 {id:"p1m6q4", q:"During a 128 KB NVMe read, which step actually moves the 128 KB of data into the destination buffer in RAM?", options:["The CPU loads it word by word from the drive's BAR registers using memory-mapped I/O","The driver polls the doorbell register and copies each completed page","The SSD writes it into the buffer by DMA, then posts a completion and interrupts","The chipset copies it once the drive raises its legacy IRQ line"], answer:2, why:"The driver only writes a 64-byte command and rings the doorbell; the SSD fetches the command, writes the 128 KB straight into RAM by DMA, posts a 16-byte completion and raises an MSI-X interrupt. MMIO is for programming control registers, not for moving bulk data."},
 {id:"p1m6q5", q:"A new board with no CSM (legacy mode) gets an old Windows disk that was installed in legacy BIOS mode on an MBR partition table. Firmware loads, then reports no bootable device. Why?", options:["UEFI launches an EFI loader from an ESP, normally on GPT, and this disk has none","The TPM has not measured the old disk, so the firmware refuses to start it","Secure Boot has added the old Windows loader to the revoked dbx list","MBR disks cannot exceed 2 TiB, so the firmware cannot read the partition"], answer:0, why:"Without CSM the firmware can only run EFI executables from an EFI System Partition; a legacy MBR install has no ESP, so nothing qualifies. Converting the disk to GPT with an ESP fixes it. The TPM records boot measurements rather than blocking boot, and the 2 TiB limit concerns size, not bootability."}
],
"p1m7":[
 {id:"p1m7q1", q:"A DUV immersion scanner (193 nm, NA 1.35) reaches k1 = 0.30, so CD = k1 x wavelength / NA = about 43 nm per exposure. A layer needs a 20 nm half-pitch. Which approach can print it?", options:["Raise k1 to about 0.6, which sharpens the printed image","Switch to a dry lens with NA 0.93 for a cleaner exposure","Expose longer at a higher dose so the light resolves finer lines","Multipatterning with DUV, or a single EUV exposure at about 16 nm"], answer:3, why:"CD shrinks only with a smaller k1 or wavelength or a larger NA, so raising k1 or using a dry lens enlarges features, and dose does not move the diffraction limit. Fabs split the layer into several DUV exposures, at extra cost and defects, or use EUV: 0.4 x 13.5 / 0.33 = about 16 nm."},
 {id:"p1m7q2", q:"Defect density is 0.2 per cm². Using Y = e^(-D x A), compare the yield of a 300 mm² monolithic die with that of each 150 mm² chiplet that replaces it.", options:["About 40% for the die and 70% for each chiplet","About 55% for the die and 74% for each chiplet","About 94% for the die and 97% for each chiplet","About 55% for both, since the defect density is the same"], answer:1, why:"300 mm² is 3 cm², so Y = e^(-0.6) = about 55%; each 1.5 cm² chiplet gets e^(-0.3) = about 74%. 94% and 97% come from treating 300 mm² as 0.3 cm², and 40% and 70% from a linear 1 - D x A instead of the Poisson model."},
 {id:"p1m7q3", q:"A 400 mm² die on a 300 mm wafer gives candidates = pi x 150² / 400 - pi x 300 / sqrt(800), and defect density is 0.1 per cm². About how many fully working dies does each wafer yield?", options:["About 177","About 118","About 96","About 143"], answer:2, why:"pi x 22,500 / 400 = 177, minus an edge loss of 942 / 28.3 = 33, leaves about 143 candidates; yield is e^(-0.4) = 67%, so about 96 good dies. 118 applies yield but skips the edge loss, and 143 forgets yield entirely."},
 {id:"p1m7q4", q:"Why did the industry abandon the planar transistor for the FinFET around 22 nm, and later move to gate-all-around nanosheets?", options:["To wrap the gate around more of the channel so it can still shut off leakage","To make the gate physically 3 nm long, matching the node names on the industry roadmap","To raise the supply voltage again, so clocks could keep climbing as before","To suit EUV lithography, which cannot print flat transistor structures"], answer:0, why:"Below about 30 nm a gate sitting on a flat channel could no longer turn it fully off, so current leaked. Wrapping the gate around three sides (FinFET), then all four (GAA), restores control of the channel. Node names are generation labels, not gate lengths."},
 {id:"p1m7q5", q:"A GB202 die tests with 180 of its 192 streaming multiprocessors working, but it needs above-average voltage to hold its clocks. What does the manufacturer typically do with it?", options:["Scrap it, since any defective unit makes a die unsellable","Sell it as an RTX 5090 Laptop GPU, where flawed dies go","Repair the faulty units with a microcode update before sale","Disable units down to 170 SMs and sell it as a desktop RTX 5090"], answer:3, why:"Binning sorts dies by working units and voltage: the desktop 5090 enables 170 of 192 SMs, so this die qualifies. The laptop 5090 is a different, smaller GB203-class die, and it is the best low-voltage parts, not flawed ones, that become laptop chips."}
],
"p1m8":[
 {id:"p1m8q1", q:"A CPU dissipates 250 W through a cooler whose junction-to-air thermal resistance is 0.32 °C/W, in a 30 °C room. What happens?", options:["It runs at about 80 °C, comfortably inside its limit","About 110 °C, so it throttles unless the cooler reaches 0.26 °C/W","It reaches about 110 °C and is damaged before the firmware can react","It runs at about 110 °C, which is normal, since desktop CPUs are rated to 125 °C"], answer:1, why:"Delta T = P x theta = 250 x 0.32 = 80 °C above a 30 °C ambient, or 110 °C, past the 95-105 °C limit, so firmware cuts clocks. Holding 95 °C needs (95 - 30) / 250 = 0.26 °C/W. 80 °C forgets the ambient; throttling is protection, not damage."},
 {id:"p1m8q2", q:"A workstation's DC load is 1,100 W on a PSU about 92% efficient. The owner wants to share its 15 A, 120 V circuit with a 600 W space heater. Which statement is right?", options:["1,100 W at the wall, so the pair's 1,700 W stays under the 1,800 W rating of the circuit","About 1,012 W at the wall, since efficiency reduces what is drawn","About 1,196 W at the wall; the pair's 1,796 W is fine under 1,800 W","About 1,196 W at the wall; with the heater, well over the 1,440 W continuous limit"], answer:3, why:"Wall power = 1,100 / 0.92 = about 1,196 W, roughly 10 A. Adding 600 W gives about 1,796 W: just under 1,800 W, but well above the 80% continuous guideline of 1,440 W for a 15 A circuit, so the breaker may trip. Efficiency losses raise wall draw, never lower it."},
 {id:"p1m8q3", q:"A case must remove 600 W of heat while the air warms by only 8 °C. Using CFM = 1.76 x watts / delta T, how much real airflow does it need?", options:["About 132 CFM","About 75 CFM","About 1,056 CFM","About 43 CFM"], answer:0, why:"1.76 x 600 / 8 = 132 CFM of actual flow through the case, after filter and radiator losses. 75 CFM drops the 1.76 factor, 1,056 CFM forgets to divide by the temperature rise, and 43 CFM divides by 1.76 instead of multiplying."},
 {id:"p1m8q4", q:"A high-end GPU has started crashing under load, and the plug of its 12V-2x6 cable is browned on one side. What is the most likely mechanism and the right response?", options:["Normal heating at 600 W; add case airflow and keep using the same cable","A weak 12 V rail; switch the supply to multi-rail mode to spread the load","A partly seated plug crowded 50 A onto fewer pins; replace the cable and seat it fully","A failing GPU core; replace the card but keep the cable, since it is rated for 600 W"], answer:2, why:"600 W at 12 V is 50 A over six pins, more than 8 A each; a plug that is not fully latched crowds that current onto fewer pins, which heat and can melt. Replace the damaged cable, inspect the card's socket, seat the new plug until it clicks and avoid tight bends near it."},
 {id:"p1m8q5", q:"A customer's laptop battery has swollen enough to lift the touchpad, but the laptop still works on AC power. What should you do?", options:["Leave it plugged in on AC only, so the cell is never cycled again","Stop using and charging it, do not press it, and isolate it for recycling","Run the battery flat first, so the cell is safe to press back into place","Vent the gas through a small puncture, then refit it until a spare arrives"], answer:1, why:"Swelling means the cell is failing and can enter thermal runaway. Stop using and charging the device, never press or puncture the pack, keep it on a non-flammable surface and take it to battery recycling. Leaving it on AC keeps it charged, which is exactly the risk."}
],
"p1m9":[
 {id:"p1m9q1", q:"An intermittent fault could be in any of 32 components, and you can design tests that clear half of the remaining suspects each time. At most how many tests do you need?", options:["5, against up to 32 testing one at a time","16, against up to 32 testing one at a time","8, against up to 16 testing one at a time","6, since the last suspect also needs its own test"], answer:0, why:"Each well-chosen test halves the suspects: 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1, so log2 32 = 5 tests. Testing one by one averages 16 and can take 32. After the fifth split only one suspect remains, so no sixth test is needed to name it."},
 {id:"p1m9q2", q:"A desktop shuts off a few minutes into games. A PSU tester shows all green lights and the 12 V rail reads 12.1 V. What should you conclude?", options:["The PSU is good, so the motherboard must be the fault","The PSU is bad, since 12.1 V falls outside the ATX tolerance for the 12 V rail","The PSU is not cleared; the tester barely loads it, so test under load","Power is ruled out, so the GPU must be overheating"], answer:2, why:"A tester checks rails at almost no load; a supply can pass and still sag or trip under a gaming load. 12.1 V is well within ATX's 5% tolerance (11.4-12.6 V). Next, test under load: log power before the shutdown, cap GPU power, or try a known-good PSU."},
 {id:"p1m9q3", q:"After a desk move, a desktop with a graphics card powers on, its keyboard lights up and the Windows startup sound plays, but the monitor says 'No signal'. What should you check first?", options:["Whether the graphics card has failed, by ordering a replacement","Whether the RAM modules came loose in transit, by reseating every one of them","Whether the firmware was corrupted, by reflashing the BIOS","Whether the cable is in the GPU's port and the monitor's input is right"], answer:3, why:"The system clearly POSTs and boots, so the fault is on the display path. After a move the classic cause is a cable in the motherboard's video port or the wrong monitor input, a one-minute check. Question the obvious before costly swaps; a RAM fault would stop POST."},
 {id:"p1m9q4", q:"To cure random crashes, a technician replaces the RAM and the PSU at the same time, and the crashes stop. What is the problem with this approach?", options:["None; once the symptom stops, the fix can be documented and closed","No one knows which part was bad; restore one original at a time to find out","Both parts must have been faulty, because the fix needed both of the swaps to work","The PSU was the cause, since supplies fail more often than memory"], answer:1, why:"Changing two variables at once means the success cannot identify the cause, and a healthy part may have been replaced for nothing. Putting one original back at a time, or testing it in a known-good system, isolates the fault so the ticket records the real cause."},
 {id:"p1m9q5", q:"A desktop stutters in games, and Event Viewer logs thousands of WHEA-Logger 19 (corrected hardware error) events per hour naming the PCIe bus. The GPU sits on a riser cable. What is the best first test?", options:["Reinstall Windows, since the event log itself is corrupted","Run MemTest86, since WHEA events always mean bad RAM","Replace the GPU, since corrected errors mean the core is failing","Mount the GPU directly in the slot and recheck errors and link status"], answer:3, why:"Corrected PCIe errors mean packets are failing their CRC and being retransmitted, a classic sign of a marginal riser or a poorly seated card. Removing the riser changes one variable; then check link width and speed in GPU-Z or lspci and whether the errors stop."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p2m1":[
 {id:"p2m1q1", q:"A 1,500-byte packet crosses a 20 Mb/s link over 2,000 km of fiber (signal speed about 200,000 km/s). Ignoring processing and queuing, what is its one-way delay?", options:["About 7.3 ms","About 10.6 ms","About 10.1 ms","About 0.6 ms"], answer:1, why:"Transmission is 12,000 bits / 20 Mb/s = 0.6 ms and propagation is 2,000 km / 200,000 km/s = 10 ms, so about 10.6 ms. 10.1 ms uses bytes instead of bits for transmission, 7.3 ms uses the speed of light in vacuum, and 0.6 ms ignores distance."},
 {id:"p2m1q2", q:"A customer's iperf3 test between two Linux servers on a clean 10 Gb/s link with a 1,500-byte MTU shows about 9.41 Gb/s, and they ask where the missing 6% went. What is the right answer?", options:["A duplex mismatch is forcing retransmissions on one side","The cable is Cat6 beyond 55 m, so the link has degraded","The switch is store-and-forward, which costs about 6% of throughput","Framing and headers: 1,448 data bytes per 1,538 byte-times is 94.1%"], answer:3, why:"Each full frame carries 1,448 bytes of TCP data (with timestamps) but occupies 1,538 byte-times including headers, FCS, preamble and inter-frame gap: 1,448 / 1,538 x 10 Gb/s = 9.41 Gb/s. The link is healthy; a duplex or cable fault would show errors and far lower numbers."},
 {id:"p2m1q3", q:"A packet travels from your laptop through a switch and two routers to a web server, with no NAT on the path. What happens to its addresses along the way?", options:["The MAC addresses are rewritten at each router; the IP addresses stay the same end to end","The IP addresses are rewritten at each router; the MAC addresses stay the same end to end","Both stay the same, since routers only read the headers and forward","The TCP ports change at each hop so that each router can track the flow"], answer:0, why:"A MAC address is meaningful only on one link, so every router builds a new frame with its own source MAC and the next hop's destination MAC. The IP header keeps the end-to-end source and destination; only TTL and checksum change. Routers touch ports only when they do NAT."},
 {id:"p2m1q4", q:"A 10 Mb/s uplink serves users who each need 1 Mb/s while active but are active only 10% of the time. How many users can circuit switching and packet switching each support well?", options:["10 for both, since the link's capacity is the same either way","100 with circuits, since each user averages only 0.1 Mb/s of traffic over a day","10 with circuits; about 35 with packets, rarely more than 10 active at once","Unlimited with packets, since packets never have to wait in queues"], answer:2, why:"Circuits reserve 1 Mb/s per user whether it is used or not, so 10 users. Packet switching shares the link statistically: with 35 users the chance that more than 10 are active at the same instant is tiny. The price is occasional queuing and loss during bursts, not zero waiting."},
 {id:"p2m1q5", q:"One user's email client cannot connect, while web browsing, Teams and ping all work from the same laptop. How should you approach it?", options:["Work bottom-up from the cable and link light, since most faults are low","Work top-down: the mail app's settings, its server's DNS name, its ports","Replace the network adapter, since one failing app points to layer 1","Restart the access switch to clear out its MAC address table"], answer:1, why:"The other applications prove the link, addressing and gateway work, so the fault sits above them: the app's configuration, its server's DNS record, or a firewall rule for its ports. Bottom-up suits suspected hardware; here it would re-test layers already shown healthy."}
],
"p2m2":[
 {id:"p2m2q1", q:"Which of these MAC addresses is a randomized, locally administered unicast address, such as a phone's private Wi-Fi MAC?", options:["01:00:5E:7F:00:01","3C:22:FB:9A:01:7E","7A:4F:10:C2:88:31","9C:B6:D0:11:22:33"], answer:2, why:"The U/L flag is the second-lowest bit of the first octet: 7A is 0111 1010, so U/L = 1 and software made the address up. 01 sets the lowest (I/G) bit instead, marking a multicast group address; 3C and 9C have both flags 0, real OUI-assigned addresses."},
 {id:"p2m2q2", q:"A 24-port switch has VLAN 10 on ports 1-12 and VLAN 20 on ports 13-24. A host on port 3 sends a frame to a MAC address the switch has never seen. Where does the frame go?", options:["Out ports 1-12 except port 3, since flooding stays inside VLAN 10","Out all 23 other ports, since unknown destinations flood everywhere","Nowhere; unknown unicast is dropped until ARP fills the table","To the router, which looks the MAC up on the switch's behalf"], answer:0, why:"An unknown unicast is flooded, but only within the frame's VLAN, because each VLAN is a separate broadcast domain; the switch also learns the sender's MAC on port 3. Switches flood rather than drop unknown frames, and only a router moves traffic between VLANs."},
 {id:"p2m2q3", q:"A switch has a 370 W PoE budget and allocates power by class. You plan four Wi-Fi 7 access points at 802.3bt Type 3 and ten cameras at 802.3af. Does it fit?", options:["Yes, because the devices draw only 4 x 51 + 10 x 12.95 = 333.5 W","No: 4 x 60 + 10 x 15.4 = 394 W allocated, over the 370 W budget","Yes, because PoE budgets count only devices drawing over 30 W","No, because 802.3bt and 802.3af devices cannot share a switch"], answer:1, why:"The switch reserves what it must supply at the PSE: 60 W per Type 3 port and 15.4 W per 802.3af port, 394 W in all. The 51 W and 12.95 W figures are what arrives at the device after cable loss, not what the budget counts."},
 {id:"p2m2q4", q:"A server link works but crawls under load. The switch port, forced to 100/full, shows climbing CRC errors; the server, left on autonegotiation, reports 100/half and late collisions. What is wrong?", options:["A damaged cable, since CRC errors always mean physical damage","A broadcast storm from a switching loop that saturates the port","The server port sits in the wrong access VLAN for its subnet","A duplex mismatch; set both ends to auto, or force both identically"], answer:3, why:"With one side forced, autonegotiation on the other side fails and falls back to half duplex. The half-duplex side logs late collisions, the full-duplex side CRC errors, and throughput collapses under load. Matching the settings fixes it; a wrong VLAN gives no traffic, not slow traffic."},
 {id:"p2m2q5", q:"A 10GBASE-LR link started flapping after a patch cord was replaced. DOM shows receive power of -14.0 dBm against a receiver sensitivity of -14.4 dBm, and the new cord's green connector is mated to a blue one. What is the most likely cause?", options:["APC mated to UPC, adding loss and reflection near the sensitivity floor","Receive power too high, overloading the receiver until it is attenuated","A single-mode optic that needs multimode fiber at this distance","A duplex mismatch between the two SFPs after the cord swap"], answer:0, why:"Mating an angled green APC to a flat blue UPC leaves an air gap that costs decibels and reflects light back at the laser. -14.0 dBm is only 0.4 dB above sensitivity, so small changes make the link flap. Use the correct connector type, inspect and clean, and recheck DOM."}
],
"p2m3":[
 {id:"p2m3q1", q:"What are the network and broadcast addresses of 172.16.45.200/20?", options:["Network 172.16.45.0, broadcast 172.16.45.255","Network 172.16.40.0, broadcast 172.16.47.255","Network 172.16.32.0, broadcast 172.16.63.255","Network 172.16.32.0, broadcast 172.16.47.255"], answer:3, why:"/20 is 255.255.240.0, so the interesting octet is the third, with block size 256 - 240 = 16. 45 falls in 32-47: network 172.16.32.0, broadcast 172.16.47.255, 4,094 usable hosts. Treating it as a /24 ignores the mask bits in the third octet."},
 {id:"p2m3q2", q:"A router holds 0.0.0.0/0 via the ISP, 172.16.0.0/12 via R1 (static, AD 1), 172.16.64.0/18 via R2 (OSPF, AD 110) and 172.16.80.0/20 via R3 (RIP, AD 120). Where does it send a packet for 172.16.95.10?", options:["R1, because a static route has the most trusted distance","R3, because its /20 is the longest prefix that matches","R2, because OSPF's metric beats RIP's hop count","The ISP, because a default route catches all traffic"], answer:1, why:"172.16.80.0/20 covers 172.16.80.0-172.16.95.255, so it matches, as do the /18, the /12 and the default. The longest matching prefix always wins; administrative distance and metric only break ties between routes to the same prefix, so RIP's poor AD is irrelevant here."},
 {id:"p2m3q3", q:"You own 10.50.8.0/24 and need LANs of 60, 28 and 12 hosts plus two point-to-point router links. Which allocation is valid?", options:["/26 at .0, /27 at .80, /28 at .112, /30s at .128 and .132","/27 at .0, /27 at .32, /28 at .64, /30s at .80 and .84","/26 at .0, /27 at .64, /28 at .96, /30s at .112 and .116","/26 at .0, /27 at .64, /29 at .96, /30s at .104 and .108"], answer:2, why:"Largest first: 62 addresses need a /26 (.0-.63), 30 a /27 on a multiple of 32 (.64), 14 a /28 on a multiple of 16 (.96), then /30s at .112 and .116. A /27 cannot start at .80, a /27 holds only 30 hosts, and a /29 only 6."},
 {id:"p2m3q4", q:"An edge router should advertise one route upstream covering 192.168.16.0/24 through 192.168.23.0/24. What is the correct summary?", options:["192.168.16.0/21","192.168.16.0/20","192.168.16.0/22","192.168.0.0/19"], answer:0, why:"Third octets 16 to 23 are 0001 0000 to 0001 0111, which agree in their first five bits, so the summary is 8 + 8 + 5 = /21, starting at 16, a multiple of 8. A /20 would also claim 24-31, which this router does not have, and a /22 covers only 16-19."},
 {id:"p2m3q5", q:"Several office PCs suddenly get 192.168.0.x addresses with gateway 192.168.0.1 instead of the office's 10.1.10.0/24 range, and they cannot reach anything. Others are fine. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The DHCP scope is exhausted, so clients self-assign addresses","The DHCP relay for that VLAN has been removed from the router","A rogue DHCP server, such as a home router, is answering first","Duplicate static IPs inside the scope are causing conflicts"], answer:2, why:"A consumer router plugged in by its LAN port answers Discovers with its own 192.168.0.x scope and gateway, and some clients accept its offer. Exhaustion or a missing relay produces 169.254 addresses instead. Remove the device, then enable DHCP snooping so only trusted ports may answer."}
],
"p2m4":[
 {id:"p2m4q1", q:"A Reno-style TCP flow uses an MSS of 1,460 bytes on a path with a 50 ms RTT and 0.04% loss (square root of p = 0.02). Using the Mathis approximation, about what throughput can it sustain on a 1 Gb/s link?", options:["About 14 Mb/s","About 712 Mb/s","About 1.8 Mb/s","The full 1 Gb/s, since loss that low is negligible"], answer:0, why:"MSS / RTT = 11,680 bits / 0.05 s = 233.6 kb/s, times 1.22 / 0.02 = 61, gives about 14 Mb/s. 712 Mb/s forgets the square root, and 1.8 Mb/s leaves the MSS in bytes. A loss rate unnoticeable on a LAN caps a long flow far below the link rate."},
 {id:"p2m4q2", q:"A client's initial sequence number is 7000 and the server's is 30000. After the handshake, the client sends one 1,200-byte segment. What sequence number does it carry, and what does the server acknowledge?", options:["seq=7000, then ack=8200","seq=30001, then ack=31201","seq=7001, then ack=8201","seq=7001, then ack=8202"], answer:2, why:"The SYN consumes one sequence number, so the first data byte is 7001. ACKs name the next byte expected: 7001 + 1,200 = 8201. Forgetting that the SYN uses a number gives 7000 and 8200; 30001 belongs to the server's own byte stream."},
 {id:"p2m4q3", q:"From an admin workstation, connecting to a database server on TCP 5432 returns 'Connection refused' immediately, while SSH to the same server works. What is the most likely explanation?", options:["A firewall is silently dropping 5432, so a rule must be opened","The server is down or has no route back to the workstation","The database is listening but its TLS handshake is failing","The host answered with a RST: nothing is listening on 5432"], answer:3, why:"Refused means a RST came back to the SYN: the host is up and reachable, but no process listens on that port, so check the service and its bind address with ss -tlnp. A silent firewall drop or a dead host produces a timeout instead."},
 {id:"p2m4q4", q:"On a path that should carry 500 Mb/s, ss -ti shows: cubic rtt:40/1.5 mss:1448 cwnd:20 ssthresh:16 retrans:0/12. What is the flow's current ceiling, and what should you investigate?", options:["About 58 Mb/s; CUBIC is still ramping up, so simply wait","About 5.8 Mb/s; ssthresh and 12 retransmissions point to packet loss","About 5.8 Mb/s; the receive window is the limit, so raise the buffers","About 0.7 Mb/s; the 1.5 ms RTT variation shows jitter is the cause"], answer:1, why:"In flight: 20 x 1,448 = 28,960 bytes, x 8 / 0.040 s = about 5.8 Mb/s. A set ssthresh means a loss has already cut the window, and 12 retransmissions confirm it, so look for loss such as a dirty connector or a marginal radio. 0.7 Mb/s forgets to convert bytes to bits."},
 {id:"p2m4q5", q:"A firewall team blocks outbound UDP 443 to simplify its rules. What will users of HTTP/3-capable sites most likely notice?", options:["Those sites fail entirely, since port 443 is now blocked","Nothing at all, since QUIC actually runs over TCP 443","All HTTPS breaks, since TLS 1.3 now travels only inside QUIC","Pages still load, a little slower, as browsers fall back to TCP"], answer:3, why:"HTTP/3 runs on QUIC, which is built on UDP. With UDP 443 blocked, browsers quietly fall back to HTTP/2 over TCP 443 with TLS, so the symptom is slightly slower pages rather than errors. TLS 1.3 still runs over TCP for everything that is not QUIC."}
],
"p2m5":[
 {id:"p2m5q1", q:"A shop's A record has a TTL of 43,200 s (12 hours), and the server moves to a new address on Saturday at 06:00. What is the latest time you can lower the TTL to 300 and still have every cache honoring the short TTL at the cutover?", options:["Saturday at 05:55, just before the change","Friday at 18:00, one full old TTL before the change","Thursday at 06:00, since DNS changes take 48 hours to propagate","Saturday at 06:00, together with the new address"], answer:1, why:"A resolver that cached the record just before you lowered the TTL keeps it for up to 12 hours, so the short TTL must be published at least one old TTL early: Friday 18:00. Nothing propagates; caches simply expire, so the 48-hour figure is a myth."},
 {id:"p2m5q2", q:"dig www.shop.example against your validating resolver returns status SERVFAIL, but dig +cd (checking disabled) against the same resolver returns the A record. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The zone's DNSSEC signatures are broken or expired","The name does not exist, so a record must be created","The resolver is offline and cannot reach any server","An old record is still cached with a long TTL"], answer:0, why:"+cd tells the resolver to skip DNSSEC validation; if the answer appears only then, the data is reachable but its signature chain fails, so the resolver returns SERVFAIL rather than a possibly forged answer. A missing name returns NXDOMAIN, and an offline resolver answers neither query."},
 {id:"p2m5q3", q:"curl -v https://portal.example.com prints '* Trying 198.51.100.40:443...' and then, after 30 seconds, 'Connection timed out'. Which layer has failed?", options:["DNS, since the name never resolved to an address","TLS, since the server's certificate chain could not be verified by curl","TCP, since DNS worked but the handshake to port 443 never completed","HTTP, since the server returned an error status code"], answer:2, why:"'Trying' with an IP address means DNS already answered. No 'Connected' line follows, so the SYN got no reply: a firewall drop, a routing problem or a host that is down. TLS and HTTP lines would appear only after the TCP connection is established."},
 {id:"p2m5q4", q:"A Python script and curl both fail against an internal HTTPS site with 'unable to get local issuer certificate', but Chrome on the same machine loads it without warnings. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The client's clock is wrong, so the certificate is not yet valid","The certificate's name does not match the site's hostname","The certificate has expired and must be renewed today","The server is not sending its intermediate certificate in the chain"], answer:3, why:"Browsers often fill in a missing intermediate from their cache or by fetching it, while curl and most libraries need the server to send the full chain. A wrong clock, a name mismatch or an expired certificate would fail in Chrome too, with a different error."},
 {id:"p2m5q5", q:"A web app sits behind a reverse proxy. Users get '504 Gateway Timeout' only on the monthly-reports page; every other page loads normally. What does that indicate?", options:["The proxy cannot reach the backend at all, so the app server is down","The proxy reached the backend, but it did not answer that request in time","The reports page does not exist on the server, so the path is wrong","The users are not authenticated for the reports page or its data"], answer:1, why:"504 means the gateway reached the backend and waited, but no answer came in time, so look at the slow work behind that page, such as a heavy query. An unreachable backend gives 502, a missing page 404 and missing authentication 401; other pages loading shows the backend is up."}
],
"p2m6":[
 {id:"p2m6q1", q:"Two offices are 2,400 km apart in a straight line, and the fiber route between them runs about 1.4 times that distance. What is the best possible round-trip time over that fiber?", options:["About 24 ms","About 17 ms","About 34 ms","About 22 ms"], answer:2, why:"The fiber path is 2,400 x 1.4 = 3,360 km; at 200,000 km/s a round trip covers 6,720 km in 33.6 ms. 24 ms applies 1 ms per 100 km to the straight-line distance, 17 ms is one way, and 22 ms assumes light at vacuum speed."},
 {id:"p2m6q2", q:"An ISP sells 1 Gb/s plans on GPON (2.488 Gb/s downstream) with 1:32 splits. At peak, 20 of the 32 subscribers on one tree download at full speed at the same time. About what does each get?", options:["1 Gb/s each, since every plan has reserved capacity","About 78 Mb/s, the port divided among all 32 subscribers","About 50 Mb/s, once GPON's framing overhead is removed","About 124 Mb/s, the port shared among the 20 active users"], answer:3, why:"The OLT port's 2.488 Gb/s is shared by the whole splitter tree, so 20 simultaneous downloads split it to about 124 Mb/s each, slightly less after overhead. Idle subscribers use nothing, so dividing by all 32 is the worst case; plans rely on statistical multiplexing."},
 {id:"p2m6q3", q:"Your company originates a /22 in BGP. Another AS starts announcing one /24 inside it. What happens to traffic for that /24, and what limits the damage?", options:["Routers prefer the more specific /24; a ROA capped at /22 makes it RPKI-invalid","Nothing, since BGP keeps the oldest announcement for any address range","Traffic splits evenly, since BGP load-balances overlapping routes","Your /22 wins wherever its AS path is shorter, so the bogus /24 stays local to its origin"], answer:0, why:"Forwarding uses longest-prefix match, so any network that accepts the /24 sends that traffic to the other AS whatever the AS paths are, as in the 2008 YouTube hijack. A ROA authorizing only your AS up to /22 lets networks doing origin validation drop the /24."},
 {id:"p2m6q4", q:"An ISP learns two routes to the same prefix: one from a paying customer with an AS_PATH of 4 ASes, one from its transit provider with an AS_PATH of 2. With typical policy, which does it choose?", options:["The provider's route, since the shorter AS path always wins the BGP decision","The customer's route, since LOCAL_PREF is compared before AS_PATH length","Whichever route has lower latency, since BGP measures delay","Both, since BGP balances load across different neighbors"], answer:1, why:"Operators give customer routes a higher LOCAL_PREF (customers pay, providers charge), and LOCAL_PREF is the first step of the decision process, ahead of AS_PATH length. BGP measures neither latency nor bandwidth, so the chosen path can be the slower one."},
 {id:"p2m6q5", q:"A service receives 400,000 requests per second at its CDN edge. Its cache hit ratio improves from 90% to 98%. What happens to the load reaching the origin?", options:["It falls from 40,000 to 8,000 requests per second","It falls by about 8%, matching the gain in hit ratio","It rises from 360,000 to 392,000 requests per second","It falls from 40,000 to 32,000 requests per second"], answer:0, why:"Only misses reach the origin: 10% of 400,000 is 40,000, and 2% is 8,000. A small change in hit ratio is a large change in miss ratio, which is why cache tuning matters so much. 360,000 and 392,000 are the hits, which are served at the edge."}
],
"p2m7":[
 {id:"p2m7q1", q:"Colleagues get 'connection refused' when they browse to a developer's app on port 8080, even though the host firewall allows 8080. On the server, ss -tulpn shows: tcp LISTEN 0 511 127.0.0.1:8080 0.0.0.0:*. What is the fix?", options:["Open port 8080 inbound on the edge firewall as well as the host","Restart the app, since its listener has crashed and must reload","Add a static route to the server's subnet on each colleague's PC","Bind the app to 0.0.0.0 or the server's LAN address, not 127.0.0.1"], answer:3, why:"127.0.0.1:8080 means the app accepts connections only from the server itself; packets to the LAN address find no listener and draw a RST, the refused error. The fix is the service's bind setting. The firewall already allows the port, and the listener is clearly running."},
 {id:"p2m7q2", q:"Through a tunnel, ping -M do -s 1372 10.10.0.5 succeeds, but -s 1373 and anything larger fail. What is the path MTU, and how much is the tunnel taking from a 1,500-byte path?", options:["1,372 bytes, so the tunnel takes 128 bytes","1,400 bytes, so the tunnel takes 100 bytes","1,380 bytes, so the tunnel takes 120 bytes","1,500 bytes, so the tunnel takes nothing"], answer:1, why:"-s sets the ICMP data only; add 8 bytes of ICMP header and 20 of IPv4 header: 1,372 + 28 = 1,400 bytes is the largest packet that passes, so the tunnel consumes 100 bytes. Reading the -s value directly as the MTU forgets those 28 header bytes."},
 {id:"p2m7q3", q:"Pinging 192.168.1.80 from 192.168.1.57/24 returns 'Reply from 192.168.1.57: Destination host unreachable'. What does that tell you?", options:["Your own host got no ARP reply for 192.168.1.80 on the local subnet","A router upstream reported that it has no route to the destination","The target is up but its firewall blocks ICMP echo requests","A routing loop made the packet's TTL expire in transit"], answer:0, why:"The reply comes from your own address, so the packet never left: ARP for an on-subnet target got no answer, meaning the host is off, unplugged or not really on this segment. A blocking firewall gives 'Request timed out', and a loop gives 'TTL expired in transit'."},
 {id:"p2m7q4", q:"VoIP phones mark their packets DSCP 46 (EF), yet calls still break up every evening when the office's internet uplink is saturated. What is most likely missing?", options:["More bandwidth, since QoS cannot help a saturated link","Marking on the PCs too, so all traffic carries DSCP 46","A priority-queue policy on the uplink's egress, the actual bottleneck","A switch to TCP for voice, so that any lost packets get retransmitted in time"], answer:2, why:"Marking only labels packets; it helps where a queue policy at the congested interface sends EF first. Without that policy, voice waits behind bulk traffic. Marking everything 46 erases the distinction, and retransmitted voice arrives too late to play."},
 {id:"p2m7q5", q:"A file server is backed up nightly at 01:00. It fails at 17:00, and service is restored from that backup at 21:00. Which statement is correct?", options:["4 hours of data was lost (the RPO) and 16 hours of service (the RTO)","The MTBF was 16 hours and the MTTR was 20 hours","Up to 16 hours of data is lost (the RPO) and 4 hours of service (the RTO)","No data was lost, since the backup ran earlier the same day"], answer:2, why:"Everything written between 01:00 and 17:00 existed only on the failed server, so up to 16 hours of work is lost; the backup schedule sets the RPO. The 4 hours from failure to restored service is measured against the RTO. The first option swaps the two."}
],
"p2m8":[
 {id:"p2m8q1", q:"What is the RFC 5952 canonical form of 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0a00:0000:0001?", options:["2001:db8::a00:0:1","2001:db8::a00::1","2001:db8:0:0:0:a00::1","2001:db8::a:0:1"], answer:0, why:"Drop leading zeros in each group, then replace the longest run of zero groups (the three after db8) with ::, leaving the lone zero group as 0. Two :: are ambiguous and illegal, :: may not replace a single zero group, and the trailing zeros in a00 are significant."},
 {id:"p2m8q2", q:"A NIC's MAC address is 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E. What link-local address does modified EUI-64 produce?", options:["fe80::1a:2bff:fe3c:4d5e","fe80::21a:2b3c:4d5e:fffe","fe80::21a:2bff:fe3c:4d5e","fe80::21a:2bfe:ff3c:4d5e"], answer:2, why:"Split the MAC, insert FFFE in the middle (001A2B FFFE 3C4D5E) and flip the universal/local bit, so 00 becomes 02: 021a:2bff:fe3c:4d5e, written fe80::21a:2bff:fe3c:4d5e. Forgetting the flip leaves 1a, and the ff:fe always sits in the middle."},
 {id:"p2m8q3", q:"An ISP delegates 2001:db8:5:c00::/56 to a small office's router. How many /64 subnets can it advertise, and what is their range?", options:["16 subnets, 2001:db8:5:c00::/64 to 2001:db8:5:c0f::/64","8 subnets, since 64 - 56 = 8","65,536 subnets, 2001:db8:5:c00::/64 to 2001:db8:5:ffff::/64","256 subnets, 2001:db8:5:c00::/64 to 2001:db8:5:cff::/64"], answer:3, why:"64 - 56 = 8 subnet bits, and 2 to the 8th is 256, so the last two hex digits of the fourth group run from 00 to ff: c00 through cff. 8 is the bit count, not the subnet count; 16 subnets would be a /60 and 65,536 a /48."},
 {id:"p2m8q4", q:"A security team blocks all ICMPv6 on every host firewall to 'reduce attack surface'. What happens to IPv6 on those hosts?", options:["Only ping6 stops working, which is harmless for normal traffic","Neighbor and router discovery and Packet Too Big fail, breaking IPv6","Nothing, since IPv6 resolves neighbors with ARP just as IPv4 does","Only DHCPv6 fails, since DHCPv6 is carried inside ICMPv6"], answer:1, why:"Neighbor and router solicitations and advertisements, duplicate address detection and Packet Too Big are all ICMPv6, and IPv6 routers never fragment, so hosts can no longer find neighbors, gateways or path MTUs. Permit what RFC 4890 lists. DHCPv6 runs over UDP 546 and 547."},
 {id:"p2m8q5", q:"On a dual-stack laptop, curl -4 https://intranet.example connects instantly, curl -6 to the same name hangs until it times out, and browsers feel only a fraction of a second slower on first connect. What is the most likely explanation?", options:["The name has no AAAA record, so every IPv6 lookup fails","The IPv4 path is congested, which slows every first connection the browser makes","NAT64 is translating all of the laptop's traffic to IPv4","A broken IPv6 path, hidden by Happy Eyeballs falling back to IPv4"], answer:3, why:"Browsers try IPv6 first and start IPv4 if it has not connected within about 250 ms, so a broken IPv6 path costs a fraction of a second rather than an outage. curl -6 has no fallback and exposes the fault. A missing AAAA would fail at once with a resolution error, not hang."}
],
"p2m9":[
 {id:"p2m9q1", q:"An ACL line must match every address in 172.20.8.0/21. Which wildcard mask goes with 172.20.8.0?", options:["255.255.248.0","0.0.7.255","0.0.8.255","0.0.0.255"], answer:1, why:"A wildcard mask is the inverse of the subnet mask: /21 is 255.255.248.0, and 255 - 248 = 7, so 0.0.7.255 ignores the 11 host bits. Writing the subnet mask itself reverses the meaning, and 0.0.8.255 uses the block size instead of the block size minus one."},
 {id:"p2m9q2", q:"An ACL reads, top to bottom: (1) permit tcp 10.20.0.0/16 to any port 443; (2) deny tcp 10.20.5.0/24 to any port 443, log; (3) permit tcp 10.20.5.0/24 to any port 22; then the implicit deny. What happens to HTTPS from 10.20.5.40?", options:["Rule 1 permits it, so the deny in rule 2 is shadowed and never fires","Rule 2 denies and logs it, since the more specific line wins","Rule 3 permits it, since that rule names the 10.20.5.0/24 subnet","The implicit deny drops it, since two of the rules conflict"], answer:0, why:"ACLs use first match, not longest match: 10.20.5.40 is inside 10.20.0.0/16, so rule 1 permits it and evaluation stops. The intended deny must sit above the broad permit. Longest-prefix logic belongs to routing tables, not to ordered rule lists."},
 {id:"p2m9q3", q:"A Linux edge router serves 600 users who each hold about 300 flows at peak, and nf_conntrack_max is 131,072. What happens at peak?", options:["Every existing connection drops the moment the table overflows","Nothing, since idle entries expire quickly enough to keep plenty of room free","About 180,000 flows overflow it; new flows fail while old ones survive","Only UDP traffic suffers, since TCP flows need no state entry"], answer:2, why:"600 x 300 = 180,000 tracked flows, well above 131,072. Once the table is full the kernel logs 'table full, dropping packet' and new flows cannot get an entry, while flows already tracked continue. Established TCP entries last days by default, and TCP needs state as much as UDP."},
 {id:"p2m9q4", q:"A WireGuard tunnel runs over an IPv6 outer path with a 1,500-byte MTU. What tunnel MTU avoids fragmentation, and what TCP MSS fits IPv4 traffic inside it?", options:["1,440-byte tunnel MTU and an MSS of 1,400","1,420-byte tunnel MTU and an MSS of 1,460","1,460-byte tunnel MTU and an MSS of 1,420","1,420-byte tunnel MTU and an MSS of 1,380"], answer:3, why:"Over IPv6, WireGuard adds 40 (outer IP) + 8 (UDP) + 16 (header) + 16 (tag) = 80 bytes, so 1,500 - 80 = 1,420. Inside, the IPv4 and TCP headers take 40 more, so the MSS is 1,380. 1,440 is the figure for an IPv4 outer path."},
 {id:"p2m9q5", q:"On Monday, ssh to a production server prints 'REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED'. Nobody announced a rebuild. What is the right response?", options:["Stop and verify the new fingerprint out of band before connecting","Delete the old known_hosts line and reconnect, since host keys rotate","Switch to password login, since the key mismatch blocks key authentication","Move SSH to port 2222 so that the warning stops appearing"], answer:0, why:"A changed host key means either the server was rebuilt or something is intercepting the connection. Confirm the fingerprint independently, for example with ssh-keygen -lf on the server's console, before typing anything; only then update known_hosts. Deleting the entry blindly trusts whoever answers."}
]
});
